# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows but its shell scripts, Dockerfiles, and
# CI workflows all execute on Linux. Git's autocrlf handling will happily check a
# shell script out with CRLF endings, and Linux then fails to run it with an
# error that names the interpreter rather than the real cause:
#
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# Forcing LF on everything that is executed by a POSIX shell, or parsed by
# Docker or GitHub Actions, prevents that entirely.

# Let Git decide for ordinary text, but normalise to LF in the repository.
* text=auto

# Must be LF: executed by a POSIX shell or parsed by a Linux toolchain.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf

# Must be CRLF: PowerShell on Windows is tolerant of LF, but keeping these
# native avoids noisy diffs for the people editing them on Windows.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch these, and never try to diff them as text.
*.pdf           binary
*.onnx          binary
*.pth           binary
*.zip           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ipynb         -text
